{"records":[{"id":"vfpfxkbcbd","title":"CtIP-mediated fork protection synergizes with BRCA1 to suppress genomic instability upon DNA replication stress. Przetocka et al","description":"In this study, we report a previously unrecognized function of CtIP in maintaining replication fork stability that is distinct from its role in DSB resection. In brief, we show that CtIP keeps DNA2 nuclease in check to limit degradation of stalled forks. Moreover, we find that loss of CtIP in BRCA1-deficient cells aggravates replication stress-induced genomic instability, causing synthetic lethality.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-09-13T15:51:57.797Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/vfpfxkbcbd","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/vfpfxkbcbd"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Alessandro","last_name":"Sartori","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Molecular Biology"],"journal_issn":["10972765"]},{"id":"35jfgtzkk4","title":"The Dystonia Gene THAP1 Controls DNA Double Strand Break Repair Choice. Shinoda et al","description":"The Shieldin complex, consisting of SHLD1, SHLD2, SHLD3 and REV7, shields double strand DNA breaks (DSBs) from nucleolytic resection. The end-protecting activity of Shieldin promotes productive non-homologous end joining (NHEJ) in G1 but can threaten genome integrity during S-phase by blocking homologous recombination (HR). Curiously, the penultimate Shieldin component, SHLD1 is one of the least abundant mammalian proteins. Here, we report that the transcription factors THAP1, YY1 and HCF1 bind directly to the SHLD1 promoter, where they cooperatively maintain the low basal expression of SHLD1. Functionally, this transcriptional network ensures that SHLD1 protein levels are kept in check to enable a proper balance between end protection and end resection during physiological DSB repair. In the context of BRCA1 deficiency, loss of THAP1 dependent SHLD1 expression confers cross resistance to PARP inhibitor and cisplatin, and shorter progression free survival in ovarian cancer patients. Consistently, we show that embryonic lethality and PARPi sensitivity of BRCA1 deficient mice is rescued by ablation of SHLD1. In contrast, loss of THAP1 in BRCA2 deficient cells increases genome instability and correlates with improved responses to chemotherapy. In summary, our study provides the first example of a transcriptional network that directly controls DSB repair choice and a potential link between pathogenic THAP1 mutations, found in patients with the neurodevelopmental movement disorder adult-onset torsion dystonia type 6 (DYT6), and DNA damage.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-04-06T07:22:29.963Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/35jfgtzkk4","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/35jfgtzkk4"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Kenta","last_name":"Shinoda","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Dali","last_name":"Zong","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Elsa","last_name":"Callen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wei","last_name":"Wu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lavinia","last_name":"Dumitrache","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Frida ","last_name":"Belinky","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Raj","last_name":"Chari","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nancy ","last_name":"Wong","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Momoko","last_name":"Ishikawa","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Andre","last_name":"Stanlie","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Trisha","last_name":"Multhaupt-Buell","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nutan","last_name":"Sharma","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Laurie","last_name":"Ozelius","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Michelle","last_name":"Ehrlich","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Peter","last_name":"McKinnon","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Andre","last_name":"Nussenzweig","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["DNA Repair"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"zn77xfkyzw","title":"Raw data repository of 'EXO1 protects BRCA1-deficient cells against toxic DNA lesions'","description":"All raw data included in figures and uncropped Western blots of the manuscript 'EXO1 protects BRCA1-deficient cells against toxic DNA lesions'.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-01-29T19:04:29.973Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/zn77xfkyzw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/zn77xfkyzw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Sylvie","last_name":"Noordermeer","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Molecular Biology","DNA Repair","BRCA1 Protein"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"6wyzgw8z8k","title":"The CST complex mediates end-protection at double-strand breaks and promotes PARP inhibitor sensitivity in BRCA1-deficient cells. DOI: https://doi.org/10.1016/j.celrep.2018.04.046","description":"Original data used for plotting the figures belonging to the study \"The CST complex mediates end-protection at double-strand breaks and promotes PARP inhibitor sensitivity in BRCA1-deficient cells\". ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-05-22T12:41:39.154Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/6wyzgw8z8k","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/6wyzgw8z8k"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Sven","last_name":"Rottenberg","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Marco","last_name":"Barazas","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","DNA Repair","Olaparib","BRCA Gene"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"yx7ym3rj6s","title":"BLM and BRCA1-BARD1 coordinate complementary mechanisms of joint DNA molecule resolution","description":"The Bloom syndrome helicase BLM interacts with topoisomerase IIIα (TOP3A), RMI1 and RMI2 to form the BTR complex, which dissolves double Holliday junctions and DNA replication intermediates to promote sister-chromatid disjunction before cell division. In its absence, structure-specific nucleases like the SMX complex (comprising SLX1-SLX4, MUS81-EME1 and XPF-ERCC1) can cleave joint DNA molecules instead, but cells deficient in both BTR and SMX are not viable. Here, we identify a negative genetic interaction between BLM loss and deficiency in the BRCA1-BARD1 tumor suppressor complex. We show that this is due to a previously overlooked role for BARD1 in recruiting SLX4 to resolve DNA intermediates left unprocessed by BLM in the preceding interphase. Consequently, cells with defective BLM and BRCA1-BARD1 accumulate catastrophic levels of chromosome breakage and micronucleation, leading to cell death. Thus, we reveal mechanistic insights into SLX4 recruitment to DNA lesions, with potential clinical implications for treating BRCA1-deficient tumors.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-10-29T16:37:54.197Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/yx7ym3rj6s","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/yx7ym3rj6s"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Andrew","last_name":"Blackford","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kaima","last_name":"Tsukada","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Julius","last_name":"Bannister","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"3m7552nbs7","title":"raw data repository microscopy images 'DNA end-resection is stimulated by an interaction between BRCA1 exon 11 and TOPBP1'","description":"This entry contains all microscopy data of the article 'DNA end-resection is stimulated by an interaction between BRCA1 exon 11 and TOPBP1', published in EMBO Reports, 2026. All other source data can be found at EMBO Reports.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-08-25T09:19:06.149Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/3m7552nbs7","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/3m7552nbs7"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Sylvie","last_name":"Noordermeer","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Microscopy","DNA Damage Response"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"tz4z2syb2r","title":"DDX11 loss causes replication stress and pharmacologically exploitable DNA repair defects","description":"DDX11 encodes an iron–sulfur cluster DNA helicase required for development, mutated, and overexpressed in cancers. Here, we show that loss of DDX11 causes replication stress and sensitizes cancer cells to DNA damaging agents, including PARP inhibitors and platinum drugs. We find that DDX11 helicase activity prevents chemotherapy drug hypersensitivity and accumulation of DNA damage. Mechanistically, DDX11 acts downstream of 53BP1 to mediate homology-directed repair and RAD51 focus formation in manners nonredundant with BRCA1 and BRCA2. As a result, DDX11 down-regulation aggravates the chemotherapeutic sensitivity of BRCA1/2-mutated cancers and resensitizes chemotherapy drug–resistant BRCA1/2-mutated cancer cells that regained homologous recombination proficiency. The results further indicate that DDX11 facilitates recombination repair by assisting DSB resection and the loading of both RPA and RAD51 on single-stranded DNA substrates. We propose DDX11 as a potential target in cancers by creating pharmacologically exploitable DNA repair vulnerabilities.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-04-29T14:06:07.979Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/tz4z2syb2r","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/tz4z2syb2r"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Nanda Kumar","last_name":"Jegadesan","orcid":"0000-0002-2710-0656"},{"first_name":"Dana","last_name":"Branzei","orcid":"0000-0002-0544-4888"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Bioinformatics","Chemical Screening","Western Blot","Immunocytochemistry","Fluorescence Imaging","Cytotoxicity Assay","Fluorescence Activated Cell Sorting","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"96nzdf5s88","title":"Supp Material Differential immunomodulatory Effect of PARP Inhibition in BRCA1 deficient and competent tumor cells","description":"Supplementary Material to \"Differential immunomodulatory Effect of PARP Inhibition in BRCA1 deficient and competent tumor cells\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-12-14T10:19:36.093Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/96nzdf5s88","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/96nzdf5s88"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Isabel","last_name":"Alvarado-Cruz","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mariam ","last_name":"Mahmoud","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mohammed ","last_name":"Khan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Shilin ","last_name":"Zhao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sebastian ","last_name":"Oeck","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rithy ","last_name":"Meas","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kaylyn ","last_name":"Clairmont","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Victoria ","last_name":"Quintana","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ying ","last_name":"Zhu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Angelo ","last_name":"Porciuncula","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hailey ","last_name":"Wyatt","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Shuangge ","last_name":"Ma","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yu ","last_name":"Shyr","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yong ","last_name":"Kong","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Patricia ","last_name":"LoRusso","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Daniel ","last_name":"Laverty","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zachary D. ","last_name":"Nagel","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kurt A","last_name":"Schalper","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Michael ","last_name":"Krauthammer","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Joann B.","last_name":"Sweasy","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Breast Cancer","Poly ADP Ribose Polymerase","Immunotherapy"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hndkgw35jb","title":"Hyperactive end joining repair mediates resistance to DNA damaging therapy in p53-deficient cells","description":"Single-cell tracking and foci counting of si-Control vs. si-TP53 treated RPE1 cells. Cells are also divided by exposure to DNA Damaging Agents (NCS  +/- DNA-PKi). ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-12-05T03:25:25.169Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hndkgw35jb","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hndkgw35jb"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Rashmi","last_name":"Kumar","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Dennis","last_name":"Simpson","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Gaorav","last_name":"Gupta","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["DNA Damage","Cellular Imaging"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hkjz8w9c6p","title":"Histone ubiquitination by the DNA damage response is required for efficient DNA replication in unperturbed S-phase. Schmid et al.","description":"Aiming at better understanding the role of non-canonical ubiquitin-mediated events in the control of DNA replication, we made quite relevant observations on the role of key DNA damage response factors – ATM/RNF8/RNF168/53BP1 – in ensuring complete and accurate DNA replication. In a nutshell, we found that the histone ubiquitin ligase RNF168 associates with PCNA at a subset of replication factories and is required for proper DNA replication in unperturbed S phase. RNF168-deficient cells show accumulation of reversed forks and reduced fork speed, which relies on MRE11-dependent reversed fork processing. These replication phenotypes are shared with other key DDR factors, ATM, RNF8 and 53BP1, and are recapitulated in cells derived from patients affected by the RIDDLE and Ataxia Telangiectasia syndromes, caused by loss of RNF168 and ATM, respectively. RNF168-deficient cells show defects in replicating a prototype of difficult-to-replicate regions, i.e. expanded GAA repeats, previously shown to induce transient fork slowing and remodelling. These replication defects are associated with detectable chromosome abnormalities in mitosis.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-09-13T21:14:23.436Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hkjz8w9c6p","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hkjz8w9c6p"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Lorenza","last_name":"Penengo","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["DNA Replication","Chromatin","DNA Damage Response"],"journal_issn":["10972765"]}],"total_records":117394,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":98,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":117394,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":116900,"restricted":59,"embargoed":435},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2011},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":612},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":451},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":384},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":380},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":376},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":343},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":315},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":249},{"id":"https://ror.org/00k4n6c32","name":"European Commission","count":202}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":114826},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1895},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":175},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"B